  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft over the forecast area is headlined by a \nsteep ridge situated over the central CONUS. The ridge is flanked on \neither side by troughs over the coasts including a strong and \ndeepening low pressure system just off of the West Coast. The high \npressure extends all the way to the surface over the CWA and calm, \nquiet weather is expected to persist as a result. There is a subtle \npocket of low pressure over Colorado initiating some weak \nconvection, but it is not forecast to reach into Kansas. Thursday \nand Friday are forecast to continue the pattern of dry weather and \nhighs in the upper 70s into the 80s. Outside of the serene \ntemperatures, winds will be breezy out of the south at 15-25 mph. \n\nSaturday will see similar temperatures, but the system mentioned \nover the West Coast will have moved eastward towards the area. With \nsouthern advection of tropical moisture, heavy rainfall is possible \nthrough the event. From late Saturday to early Monday, chances for \nrain in the western counties stay at 30% or higher via ensembles. \nThe heaviest rainfall is expected to stay west of Highway 83 and \ninto Colorado. Rainfall is forecast for the entire CWA through the \nevent, however. The current expectation is that there will be a \nminimal to none severe threat. Event total QPF is up to around 2-\n2.25\" in of rain. A limited amount of CAPE is forecast and will \nlimit the intensity of the convection. That said the 2 inches of \nrain is expected to be drawn out along the 24-48 hour event which \nshould limit most of the widespread potential. This is primarily \nbased off the current solution that the models have trended towards \nbeing slower and later as a system. This keep most of the training \nof the rainfall in Colorado and along the border. If this continues \nand is realized, most of the flooding risk will be outside of the \nCWA. That said if the outcome shifts 50 miles eastward, there may be \nwidespread flooding west of highway 83. Otherwise the flooding will \nlikely only come in the form of localized flash flooding in urban \nand flood prone areas. Being the forecast focus, it will need to be \nmonitored in the coming days as temporal uncertainty decreases. \n\nBehind the event, cooler more seasonal temperatures are \nexpected with highs in the 60s. Outside of lingering showers on \nTuesday, no active weather is forecast through the rest of the \nforecast period.",
